#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:240
msgid "Processing the model step-by-step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:242
msgid ""
"One way of gaining more control over the simulation processing is by passing "
"options, as outlined in the `documentation <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/"
"latest/source/api/simulation.html>`__. However, you can also process the "
"model step-by-step yourself. A detailed example of this can be found in the "
"`SPM notebook <../models/SPM.ipynb>`__, but here we outline the basic steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:244
msgid "First we pick a model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "model = pybamm.lithium_ion.SPM()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:265
msgid ""
"Next we must set up the geometry. We'll use the default geometry for the "
"SPM. In all of the following steps we will also use the default settings "
"provided by the model. For a look at changing these options, see the `change "
"settings <../change-settings.ipynb>`__ notebook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "geometry = model.default_geometry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:286
msgid ""
"Both the model and geometry depend on parameters (such as the electrode "
"thickness, or diffusivity). We'll use the default model parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "param = model.default_parameter_values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:307
msgid ""
"Now that we have picked our parameters we can \"process\" the model and "
"geometry. This just means we look through the model and geometry for any "
"parameter symbols and replace them with the numeric values (or functions, in "
"the case of parameters that have functional dependence) defined by our "
"parameter values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"param.process_model(model)\n"
"param.process_geometry(geometry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:329
msgid ""
"Next we must create a mesh on which to solve the discretised equations. This "
"not only depends on the geometry, but also on the type of submesh (e.g. "
"uniformly space) and number of mesh points to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, model.default_submesh_types, model."
"default_var_pts)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:350
msgid ""
"Now that we have defined a mesh we can discretise our model. In order to do "
"so we must choose a spatial method. The default for the SPM is the Finite "
"Volume Method. We first define a discretisation, which depends on the mesh "
"and spatial method, and then use this to process our model. This turns the "
"variables in the models into a ``StateVector``, and replaces spatial "
"operators with matrix-vector multiplications, ready to be passed to a time "
"stepping algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[11]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, model.default_spatial_methods)\n"
"disc.process_model(model)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:373
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"&lt;pybamm.models.full_battery_models.lithium_ion.spm.SPM at "
"0x2699d43f100&gt;\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:379
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"<pybamm.models.full\\_battery\\_models.lithium\\_ion.spm.SPM at "
"0x2699d43f100>\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:386
msgid ""
"<pybamm.models.full_battery_models.lithium_ion.spm.SPM at 0x2699d43f100>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:397
msgid ""
"Finally, we pick a solver to step the problem forward in time. We'll use the "
"default ODE solver for the SPM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[12]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "solver = model.default_solver"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:418
msgid ""
"We then integrate in time using the ``solve`` command, as with the "
"simulation. Note that we now have to pass the model object to the ``solve`` "
"command, and that we return the solution object so that we can interact with "
"it later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[13]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "solution = solver.solve(model, [0, 3600])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:439
msgid ""
"We can create the default slider plot by passing the solution object to the "
"``dynamic_plot`` method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "[14]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:-1
msgid "pybamm.dynamic_plot(solution)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/simulations_and_experiments/simulation-class.ipynb:462
msgid ""
"<script type=\"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json\">{\"model_id\": "
"\"daf8dc7b0f524c3eb32b6a3d28f978de\", \"version_major\": 2, "
"\"version_minor\": 0}</script>"
msgstr ""

